Small arithmetic that is easy to get wrong by hand.

The overflow functions compute into an out parameter and return true when the exact result did
not fit; the out parameter then holds the wrapped value. They take any integer types, mixed,
and any number of operands, and compile to the add, sub or mul followed by one branch on the
flags (`__builtin_*_overflow`). Every size computation before an allocation in ca goes through
them: `count * sizeof(T) + header` either fits or the allocation fails cleanly.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/limits.hpp"
#include "ca/numeric.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto bytes_for(const ca::usz count, const ca::usz size, ca::usz& out) noexcept -> bool {
	return !ca::mul_overflow(out, count, size) && !ca::add_overflow(out, out, 16U);
}

static_assert([] {
	ca::usz n = 0U;
	return bytes_for(10U, 8U, n) && n == 96U;
}());

static_assert([] {
	ca::usz n = 0U;
	return !bytes_for(ca::limits<ca::usz>::max() / 2U, 4U, n);
}());

static_assert([] {
	ca::u8 out = 0U;
	return ca::add_overflow(out, 200, 100) && out == 44U;
}());

static_assert([] {
	int out = 0;
	return !ca::sub_overflow(out, 10, 3, 2) && out == 5;
}());
```

`align_up(value, alignment)` rounds value up to a multiple of a power of two, in place, and
returns true on overflow, leaving value unchanged. `ca::min` and `ca::max` return a reference
to the lesser or greater argument, the first when they are equal; both arguments have the same
type, so `ca::min(n, 4U)` needs n to be unsigned.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/numeric.hpp"

static_assert([] {
	ca::usz offset = 13U;
	return !ca::align_up(offset, 8U) && offset == 16U;
}());

static_assert(ca::max(3, 7) == 7 && ca::min(ca::usz{3U}, ca::usz{7U}) == 3U);
```
